== BriefBody (pi) ==
Agent: pi
Owned: own
Mode: implementation; edit only owned files
Verify: ver
Report: rep

Task:
task

## Leaf rules

- Never revert a change you did not make; no `git commit`, `stash`, `checkout`, `reset` or `restore`.
- Working in a lane (`~/worktrees/<repo>/<name>`): once verify and review pass, commit your owned files there with
  `claude-code worktree commit --task <id> -m <msg> <files>` before handing back; `lane-land` then lands it.
- Never a foreground `sleep`: `run_in_background` and wait for the notification, a background `until` loop, or
  `claude-code agents result -f <id>`; no `sudo` or `git switch`; read history with `git show <rev>:<path>` or `git log`.
- No `sleep` polling loop: wait with `run_in_background` and its notification, or Monitor.
- Always end with SubagentHandback when the host offers it, on every brief, audits included.
- Write file content that names a blocked command with the Write or Edit tool, never a shell heredoc.
- Never wipe or rewrite the dispatch env (`env -i`, `env -u HARNESS_DISPATCH_*`, an interpreter clearing its env):
  `safety-scan` blocks it for a leaf; hand the exact command back for the main session to run.
- CLAUDE.md: main session only (`safety-scan` blocks a leaf's write). Hand the change back to the main session (or
  message it) with the exact file path and the edit to make.
- Edit source with Edit or Write, never a splice script, `sed -i` or `perl -pi`; mutation-test a copy under `$TMPDIR`;
  no `rm -rf` outside `$TMPDIR` or scratch dirs; never print a credential variable, even piped to `grep`.
- A `python3` heredoc that rewrites a file is a splice script; write a helper script with Write, not heredoc + chmod.
- Every `rm` names a literal path: no `$(...)`, unquoted variable or glob; never `rm` in `bash -c`/`sh -c`.
- Never read/export live vault or provider config (`vault ls`, `vault get`) unless the brief names it; use fixtures.
- Edit only the owned files; a package owns the `_test.go` of each owned source file. A compile error in a file you do
  not own is reported, not fixed.
- Tests, by default: basic tests that a new feature works (a couple of seconds at most); a bug fix shows its live
  before/after run; other cases are proposals in the hand-back, as a ```proposed_tests fence in the full report (JSON lines, Go reads it there), e.g. {"pkg":"internal/x","case":"empty input → error","reason":"guards the nil path","runtime":"<5ms"}; need more, say why.
- Shell output you read goes through rtk: `rtk git|go|grep|rg|ls|find|diff|wc …`, `rtk grep -n`, `rtk git diff`, `rtk go test`; one file
  is the Read tool, never cat/head; exact bytes: `rtk bypass <cmd>`.
- Order: format → lint → live check → only a test you wrote or repair (`go test -run '^TestX$' <pkg>`) → Skill(simplify); never `./...`; package tests run in the sign-off driver.
- A relay waiting on this job: the moment `claude-code agents result -f <id>` returns with the job ended, hand back
  the job's result as your next action; do nothing in between.
- Code leaves run on the Databricks arm (`/coworker`'s default overlay `{"provider":"databricks","profile":"auto",
  "remote":"auto"}`), spread over dev/qa/prod; docs leaves on claude sonnet.

## Reporting contract

Write the full report to the Report path above.
Wrap lines at 118 columns. Include outcome, commands and exit codes, observable proof, test teeth proof,
gross production lines added and deleted, what net growth clarified, and anything blocked.
Final answer: at most 8 lines: done or not, verify result, observable proof, gross production lines added
and deleted, anything blocked, and the full report path.
Finish by calling the `handback` tool once, last, with `status` (DONE, DONE_WITH_CONCERNS, NEEDS_CONTEXT or BLOCKED),
`summary` (that final answer), `unfixed` (what you could not fix, empty if none), `tests` (executed, judged or
unknown) and `method` (solo, or fleet if you sub-contracted): the harness builds the verdict and the `UNFIXED:` line.

